course_code,question_text,choice_a,choice_b,choice_c,choice_d,correct_choice,rationale,choice_a_rationale,choice_b_rationale,choice_c_rationale,choice_d_rationale
NUR-201,"What is the normal adult resting respiratory rate?",8-12 breaths/min,12-20 breaths/min,20-30 breaths/min,30-40 breaths/min,B,"Normal adult respiratory rate is 12–20 breaths per minute. Rates below 12 indicate bradypnea; rates above 20 indicate tachypnea.","Bradypnea — too slow for a normal adult.","Correct — normal adult range.","Tachypnea — above the normal range.","Markedly tachypneic — abnormal for a resting adult.",
NUR-201,"What is the normal adult resting heart rate?",40-60 bpm,60-100 bpm,100-120 bpm,120-150 bpm,B,"Normal adult heart rate is 60–100 beats per minute. Below 60 is bradycardia; above 100 is tachycardia.","Bradycardia — abnormal for a resting adult.","Correct — normal adult range.","Tachycardia — above the normal range.","Marked tachycardia — abnormal for a resting adult.",
NUR-201,"A patient's blood pressure is 145/95 mmHg. How is this classified?",Normal,Elevated,Stage 1 hypertension,Stage 2 hypertension,C,"According to the American Heart Association, systolic 130–139 or diastolic 80–89 is Stage 1 hypertension. 145/95 falls in this range.","Normal is below 120/80.","Elevated is 120–129 systolic with diastolic below 80.","Correct — Stage 1 hypertension (130–139 or 80–89).","Stage 2 hypertension is ≥140 or ≥90.",
NUR-201,"A patient has a temperature of 39.5°C (103.1°F). What is this called?",Normal,Hypothermia,Low-grade fever,High fever (hyperpyrexia),D,"Temperatures ≥39.5°C (103.1°F) are high fever. Hyperpyrexia is ≥41°C (105.8°F) — the term is used loosely here for high fever.","Normal body temperature is 36.5–37.5°C.","Hypothermia is below 35°C.","Low-grade fever is 37.5–38°C.","Correct — high fever.",
NUR-201,"Which pulse site is MOST commonly used for routine adult assessment?",Radial,Carotid,Brachial,Popliteal,A,"The radial artery at the wrist is the most accessible and non-invasive site for routine assessment.","Correct — easily accessible, safe to palpate.","Carotid is used during emergencies (CPR), not routine.","Brachial is used for BP measurement and infants.","Popliteal is used to assess circulation to the lower leg.",
NUR-201,"The primary purpose of aseptic technique is to:",Sterilize the environment,Prevent infection transmission,Speed up wound healing,Reduce patient anxiety,B,"Aseptic technique prevents the introduction of pathogens into a susceptible site, thereby preventing infection transmission.","Complete sterilization is not achievable in a hospital ward.","Correct — the primary purpose is infection prevention.","Aseptic technique doesn't directly speed healing.","Aseptic technique is not about anxiety.",
NUR-201,"Medical asepsis is also known as:",Sterile technique,Clean technique,Surgical asepsis,Isolation technique,B,"Medical asepsis (clean technique) reduces the number of microorganisms and prevents their spread. Surgical asepsis (sterile technique) eliminates all microorganisms.","Sterile technique is surgical asepsis.","Correct — medical asepsis is clean technique.","Surgical asepsis is sterile technique, not medical asepsis.","Isolation is a specific practice within medical asepsis, not a synonym.",
NUR-201,"When should a nurse perform hand hygiene?",Only before patient contact,Only after patient contact,Before and after every patient contact,Only when visibly soiled,C,"Hand hygiene must be performed before and after every patient contact to prevent cross-contamination.","Hand hygiene is needed both before and after, not just before.","Hand hygiene is needed both before and after, not just after.","Correct — before and after every patient contact.","Hand hygiene is required even when hands are not visibly soiled.",
NUR-201,"Which of the following requires surgical asepsis?",Making a bed,Administering an oral medication,Inserting a urinary catheter,Taking a blood pressure,C,"Urinary catheter insertion introduces a foreign object into a sterile body cavity, so sterile technique is required.","Making a bed uses clean (medical asepsis) technique.","Oral medication administration uses clean technique.","Correct — catheter insertion requires sterile technique.","BP measurement uses clean technique.",
NUR-201,"A patient is on airborne precautions. Which PPE is required for entry?",Standard precautions only,Surgical mask,Surgical mask + gloves,N95 respirator + gloves + gown,D,"Airborne precautions (TB, measles) require an N95 respirator or PAPR, plus gloves and gown for entry.","Surgical mask does not filter airborne particles.","A surgical mask is inadequate for airborne precautions.","Correct — N95 plus gloves and gown.",
NUR-201,"The most important nursing intervention for preventing falls in an elderly patient is:",Restraints,Regular toileting rounds,Lights off at night,Reducing fluid intake,B,"Regular toileting rounds prevent the most common cause of falls: the patient trying to get to the bathroom unassisted.","Restraints increase injury risk and are a last resort.","Correct — scheduled toileting prevents falls.","Lights off increases fall risk; dim lighting is preferred.","Reducing fluids causes dehydration and confusion, increasing risk.",
NUR-201,"Which of the following is a modifiable fall risk factor?",Age,Previous fall,Impaired vision,History of osteoporosis,C,"Impaired vision is modifiable through corrective lenses and environmental adjustments. Age, previous falls, and osteoporosis history are non-modifiable.","Age is non-modifiable.","A previous fall is history — non-modifiable.","Correct — vision can be corrected or optimized.","Osteoporosis history is non-modifiable.",
NUR-201,"When transferring a patient from bed to chair, the nurse should:",Keep feet together,Bend at the waist to lift,Keep the patient's knees locked,Position the patient's feet flat and close to the chair,D,"The patient's feet should be flat and close to the chair to provide a stable base. The nurse should bend at the knees, not the waist.","Feet together is unstable.","Bending at the waist risks back injury.","The patient's knees should be slightly bent, not locked.","Correct — feet flat and close to the chair.",
NUR-201,"Restraints should be released and the patient reassessed every:",15 minutes,30 minutes,2 hours,4 hours,B,"Joint Commission and CMS standards require releasing restraints and reassessing every 30 minutes for violent patients and every 2 hours for non-violent, medical-surgical patients. 30 minutes is the standard acute-care reassessment interval.","15 minutes is overkill unless the patient is violent.","Correct — 30 minutes for regular reassessment.","2 hours is the maximum for some non-violent cases, but 30 minutes is the standard reassessment.","4 hours exceeds the standard reassessment interval.",
NUR-201,"The nursing process consists of five steps. Which is the FIRST step?",Diagnosis,Planning,Assessment,Evaluation,C,"The nursing process is Assessment → Diagnosis → Planning → Implementation → Evaluation. Assessment is the first and foundational step.","Diagnosis comes after assessment.","Planning comes after diagnosis.","Correct — assessment is the first step.","Evaluation is the final step.",
NUR-201,"Which data are collected during the assessment phase?",Only physical exam findings,Only patient history,Both subjective and objective data,Only lab results,C,"Assessment collects subjective data (what the patient says) and objective data (what the nurse observes and measures).","Assessment collects more than just physical findings.","History alone is incomplete.","Correct — both subjective and objective data.","Lab results are one type of objective data, not the whole picture.",
NUR-201,"A nursing diagnosis differs from a medical diagnosis because it:",Identifies the disease process,Focuses on the patient's response to health problems,Is used by physicians,Determines the treatment plan,B,"Nursing diagnoses focus on the patient's response to health problems; medical diagnoses identify the disease itself.","Identifying the disease is a medical diagnosis.","Nursing diagnoses are made by nurses.","Medical diagnoses drive the medical treatment plan.",
NUR-201,"Which is a correctly written SMART goal?",Patient will be comfortable,Patient will report pain ≤3/10 within 30 minutes of analgesia,Patient will feel better soon,Patient will be given pain medication,B,"A SMART goal is Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound. 'Report pain ≤3/10 within 30 minutes' hits all five.","Vague, not measurable.","Correct — SMART format.","Not measurable, no timeframe.","This is an intervention, not a goal.",
NUR-201,"The purpose of evaluation in the nursing process is to:",Restart the process,Determine whether the care plan met the goals,Discharge the patient,Document interventions,B,"Evaluation determines whether the interventions were effective in achieving the goals, or whether the plan needs revision.","The process may restart, but that's not the primary purpose.","Correct — evaluating goal achievement.","Discharge is a separate decision.","Documentation is part of the process, not its goal.",
NUR-201,"Which of the following is subjective data?",Heart rate 88 bpm,Patient states: 'I have a headache',Blood pressure 130/85 mmHg,Urine output 500 mL,B,"Subjective data is what the patient reports. Objective data is measurable — HR, BP, urine output.","HR is objective.","Correct — patient's own report.","BP is objective.","Urine output is objective.",
NUR-201,"The rights of medication administration include:",Right patient, right drug, right dose, right route, right time,Right patient, right drug only,Right dose only,Right route only,A,"The classic five rights: patient, drug, dose, route, time. Many institutions add documentation, reason, and response.","Incomplete — all five are required.","Incomplete — more than just dose.","Incomplete — more than just route.","Correct — the five classic rights.",
NUR-201,"When preparing a medication, if the dosage seems unusually high, the nurse should:",Administer it anyway as prescribed,Halve the dose without notifying,Verify the order with the prescriber before administering,Discard the medication,C,"Any medication order that seems unusual should be verified with the prescriber before administration. The nurse is responsible for ensuring the order is safe.","Administering without verification risks harm.","Changing a dose without authorization is a serious error.","Correct — verify with the prescriber.","Discarding without verification wastes the drug and skips the required action.",
NUR-201,"The preferred site for IM injection in an infant is:",Dorsogluteal,Ventrogluteal,Vastus lateralis,Deltoid,C,"The vastus lateralis (anterolateral thigh) is the preferred IM site for infants because it's well-developed and safe from nerve damage.","Dorsogluteal is avoided in children due to sciatic nerve risk.","Ventrogluteal is used in older children and adults.","Correct — vastus lateralis.","Deltoid is too small in infants.",
NUR-201,"The deltoid site for IM injection can accommodate up to what volume?",0.5 mL,1.0 mL,2.0 mL,3.0 mL,B,"The deltoid is small; maximum volume is 1 mL. Larger volumes should use vastus lateralis, ventrogluteal, or dorsogluteal sites.","0.5 mL is safe but under the maximum.","Correct — up to 1 mL.","2 mL exceeds deltoid capacity.","3 mL significantly exceeds deltoid capacity.",
NUR-201,"Which needle angle is used for a subcutaneous injection?",15 degrees,45 degrees,90 degrees,5 degrees,B,"Subcutaneous injections use a 45° or 90° angle depending on skin thickness; 45° is standard for most adults and used in textbooks.","15° is intradermal.","Correct — 45° (or 90° in obese patients).","90° is used for IM injections and for SC in very obese patients.","5° is not a standard injection angle.",
NUR-201,"The purpose of Z-track technique in IM injections is to:",Speed up absorption,Seal medication in the muscle,Reduce pain,Prevent nerve injury,B,"Z-track technique displaces the skin before injection, creating a Z-shaped path that seals the medication in the muscle and prevents leakage into subcutaneous tissue.","Z-track slows absorption slightly, but the main purpose is sealing.","Correct — prevents medication leakage.","Pain is not the primary benefit.","Nerve injury prevention is site-dependent, not Z-track.",
NUR-201,"Which is a correct step for administering a suppository?",Insert just past the anal sphincter,Insert 10 cm into the rectum,Insert without lubrication,Insert along the anterior wall of the rectum,A,"Suppositories are inserted just past the anal sphincter (about 3–4 cm for adults) and held in place until absorbed.","Inserting past 5 cm is only appropriate for some specific applications.","Lubrication is essential.","Correct — insert just past the sphincter.",
NUR-201,"When documenting nursing care, the nurse should:",Use pencil,Use a shared log book,Use black ink and correct errors with a single line,Leave blank lines for later,B,"Legally defensible documentation uses black ink, writes continuously (no blank lines), and corrects errors with a single line and initials — never erasures.","Pencil can be altered — never acceptable for medical records.","Shared logs violate confidentiality.","Correct — black ink and single-line correction.","Blank lines allow additions after the fact and are not permitted.",
NUR-201,"A DNR (Do Not Resuscitate) order means:",No medications,No CPR if the patient stops breathing or has no heartbeat,No surgery,No nursing care,B,"DNR specifically means no cardiopulmonary resuscitation. Other care continues as appropriate.","Medications are still given per other orders.","Correct — no CPR.","Surgery may still be performed; DNR refers to resuscitation.","Nursing care continues.",
NUR-201,"The FIRST action when a patient falls is to:",Move the patient back to bed,Assess for injuries and level of consciousness,Notify the family,Write an incident report,B,"Assess the patient first for injuries, level of consciousness, and vital signs before moving them. Never move an injured patient without assessment.","Moving an injured patient can worsen injuries.","Correct — assess first.","Family notification comes after the patient is stable.","Incident reports come after care is provided.",
NUR-201,"A nurse receives a verbal order from a physician by phone. The nurse should:",Accept and administer without documenting,Refuse all verbal orders,Document the order in the record and read it back for confirmation,Wait for written order next shift,C,"Verbal orders must be read back for confirmation, documented promptly, and signed by the physician within institution-specified timeframes.","Always document verbal orders.","Refusing all verbal orders is impractical in emergencies.","Correct — read back and document.","Waiting is unsafe — the order is needed now.",
NUR-201,"Which term describes the nurse's legal obligation to protect patients from harm?",Beneficence,Nonmaleficence,Autonomy,Justice,B,"Nonmaleficence means 'do no harm' — the nurse must avoid causing harm to patients.","Beneficence means acting to promote good.","Correct — nonmaleficence is 'do no harm.'","Autonomy is the patient's right to choose.","Justice means fair treatment.",
NUR-201,"Which of the following demonstrates autonomy?",Nurse decides treatment,Patient chooses their treatment after informed consent,Nurse protects the patient from harm,Distribution of scarce resources,B,"Autonomy is the patient's right to make informed decisions about their own care.","Nurses do not unilaterally decide treatment.","Correct — informed patient choice.","Harm prevention is nonmaleficence.","Resource distribution is justice.",
NUR-201,"A patient refuses a life-saving treatment. The nurse should:",Perform the treatment anyway,Respect the refusal after confirming understanding,Report the patient to the police,Ignore the refusal,B,"A competent patient has the right to refuse treatment, even life-saving treatment, after informed consent is confirmed.","Forcing treatment is assault and battery.","Correct — respect refusal after confirming informed choice.","Patients who refuse treatment are not criminals.","Ignoring refusal is unethical and illegal.",
NUR-201,"Which is considered a critical lab value that must be reported immediately?",Sodium 138 mEq/L,Potassium 6.5 mEq/L,Hemoglobin 13 g/dL,Glucose 95 mg/dL,B,"Potassium above 6.0 mEq/L is a critical value that can cause fatal arrhythmias and must be reported immediately.","Sodium 138 is normal (135–145).","Correct — potassium 6.5 is critical.","Hemoglobin 13 is normal for adults.","Glucose 95 is normal.",
NUR-201,"When obtaining a blood pressure reading, the patient should have:",The arm raised above the heart,The arm unsupported,The arm supported at heart level,Legs crossed,C,"Blood pressure should be measured with the arm supported at heart level, feet flat, back supported.","Arm above heart falsely lowers BP.","Unsupported arm falsely raises BP.","Correct — arm at heart level.","Crossed legs falsely raise BP.",
NUR-201,"The purpose of a pain assessment scale is to:",Distract the patient,Quantify the patient's experience in a reproducible way,Replace the need for analgesia,Standardize all patients' pain,B,"Pain scales quantify the subjective experience so it can be tracked and communicated. Different scales suit different populations (numeric, FACES, FLACC).","Pain scales don't distract — they assess.","Correct — reproducible quantification.","Pain scales don't replace analgesia.","Pain is subjective; scales reflect the individual patient's report.",
NUR-201,"Which route delivers the fastest onset of medication action?",Oral,Subcutaneous,Intramuscular,Intravenous,D,"IV medications enter the bloodstream directly and act within seconds to minutes.","Oral has a slow onset (30–60 min).","Subcutaneous onset is 15–30 minutes.","IM onset is 10–20 minutes.","Correct — IV is fastest.",
NUR-201,"The FIRST stage of wound healing is:",Proliferation,Hemostasis,Inflammation,Maturation,B,"Wound healing phases: hemostasis (immediate) → inflammation (days 1–3) → proliferation (3–21 days) → maturation (up to 1 year). Hemostasis is first — stopping bleeding.","Proliferation comes third.","Correct — hemostasis is first.","Inflammation follows hemostasis.","Maturation is the final phase.",
NUR-201,"Which finding in a wound suggests infection?",Pink wound edges,Yellow-green discharge with foul odor,Clear serous drainage,Decreased pain,B,"Purulent discharge with foul odor is a classic sign of wound infection. Other signs: warmth, redness, swelling, fever.","Pink edges suggest healthy healing.","Correct — purulent, foul-smelling discharge.","Serous drainage can be normal.","Increased pain often accompanies infection.",
NUR-201,"Pressure injuries most commonly develop over:",Fatty areas,Bony prominences,Muscle-rich areas,Joints of the hands,B,"Pressure injuries develop where bone presses against skin — heels, sacrum, elbows, hips. These are the standard risk areas.","Fatty tissue cushions pressure.","Correct — bony prominences.","Muscle-rich areas have more padding.","Joints of the hands are not common sites.",
NUR-201,"A stage 2 pressure injury is characterized by:",Intact skin with non-blanchable redness,Full-thickness skin loss,Partial-thickness skin loss with visible dermis,Exposed bone or tendon,C,"Stage 2: partial-thickness loss of dermis — shallow ulcer with pink or red wound bed.","That's stage 1.","That's stage 3 or 4.","Correct — partial-thickness loss.","That's stage 4.",
NUR-201,"The purpose of a wound dressing is to:",Replace the need for wound care,Provide a moist healing environment,Promote wound drying,Absorb all wound fluids,B,"A moist environment promotes faster epithelialization and reduces infection risk.","Dressings are part of wound care.","Correct — moist wound healing.","Drying the wound slows healing.","Absorption is one purpose, but moist healing is the primary goal.",
NUR-201,"Which sign is NOT part of the classic inflammation response?",Redness,Warmth,Swelling,Pallor,D,"Classic signs of inflammation: redness (rubor), warmth (calor), swelling (tumor), pain (dolor), loss of function (functio laesa). Pallor (paleness) is the opposite of redness.","Redness is a classic sign.","Warmth is a classic sign.","Swelling is a classic sign.","Correct — pallor is not a sign of inflammation.",
NUR-201,"When auscultating lung sounds, crackles indicate:",Fluid in the alveoli,Normal lungs,Airway obstruction,Muscle spasm,A,"Crackles (rales) are discontinuous, high-pitched sounds caused by fluid in the alveoli, typical of pneumonia and heart failure.","Normal lungs have clear breath sounds.","Correct — crackles suggest fluid.","Wheezing suggests airway obstruction.","Muscle spasm is not an auscultation finding.",
NUR-201,"Which finding in a patient's chart is concerning for a respiratory emergency?",Respiratory rate 14,SpO2 98% on room air,Stridor on inspiration,Clear lung sounds,C,"Stridor is a high-pitched inspiratory sound indicating upper airway obstruction — a medical emergency.","RR 14 is normal.","SpO2 98% is normal.","Correct — stridor indicates obstruction.","Clear sounds are normal.",
NUR-201,"The purpose of incentive spirometry is to:",Increase lung volume and prevent atelectasis,Decrease mucus production,Increase heart rate,Decrease oxygen demand,A,"Incentive spirometry encourages deep breathing, expanding the lungs and preventing atelectasis (collapse) after surgery.","It doesn't reduce mucus directly.","Correct — increases lung expansion.","Heart rate is not the target.","Oxygen demand reduction is not the target.",
NUR-201,"Which of the following is a normal adult urine output per hour?",5 mL,10 mL,30 mL,100 mL,C,"Normal adult urine output is at least 0.5 mL/kg/hr; for a 60–80 kg adult, that's 30–40 mL/hr. Output under 30 mL/hr is oliguria.","5 mL/hr indicates severe oliguria or anuria.","10 mL/hr is below normal.","Correct — 30 mL/hr is the minimum normal output.","100 mL/hr is above normal but not concerning.",
NUR-201,"The FIRST step in assessing a patient with a suspected fracture is:",Immobilize the limb,Check distal pulses and sensation,Apply ice,Give pain medication,B,"Always assess neurovascular status (pulses, sensation, capillary refill) distal to the injury before and after intervention. This catches complications early.","Immobilization comes after assessment.","Correct — check distal pulses and sensation.","Ice comes after assessment.","Pain medication is given after assessment.",
NUR-201,"When a patient has a new ostomy, the nurse should assess the stoma for:",Dark, necrotic color,Pink to red, moist appearance,Blue or black discoloration,White patches,B,"A healthy stoma is pink to red and moist. Any dark, blue, black, or pale stoma indicates ischemia and is an emergency.","Dark or necrotic color indicates ischemia.","Correct — pink to red and moist is healthy.","Blue or black indicates necrosis.","White patches may indicate candidiasis or ischemia.",
NUR-201,"The most important step in the management of a choking adult who can still cough is:",Immediate abdominal thrusts,Back blows,Encourage continued coughing and monitor,Heimlich maneuver,C,"If the patient can cough forcefully, encourage it — the cough may clear the obstruction. Only intervene if the cough becomes ineffective.","Abdominal thrusts are for ineffective cough.","Back blows are for infants and ineffective cough.","Correct — encourage the cough.","Heimlich is for ineffective cough.",
NUR-201,"The normal adult resting oxygen saturation (SpO2) is:",80-85%,85-90%,90-94%,95-100%,D,"Normal SpO2 is 95–100% on room air. Below 90% indicates hypoxemia requiring intervention.","80-85% is severe hypoxemia.","85-90% is significant hypoxemia.","90-94% is borderline and concerning.","Correct — 95-100% on room air."